WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.570
CBS News: One of the biggest sporting events
on earth.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:10.430
BBC: It’s a sporting occasion like no other.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:11.929
Vanemas: And South Korea have scored!

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:16.730
John Oliver: A country even qualified for
it is a cause for huge celebration.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:17.730
France 24: It was a moment that people were
craving.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.390
It was as if a bottle of champagne had finally
exploded and it was indeed cathartic.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.500
Two-thirds of the team are of immigrant origin.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.340
This is something we should celebrate.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.829
BBC: France beat Croatia in the final in Moscow…

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:37.200
FIFA: Spain are world champions.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.559
FIFA: Italy are champions of the world.

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:46.379
FIFA TV: It is a World Cup final the world
will never forget.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:55.420
SIERRA: The 2022 World Cup has officially
kicked off in Qatar, with 32 nations competing

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.280
for the championship title.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.750
You may have seen some of the big moments
so far - like Saudi Arabia’s crazy upset

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.129
of Argentina in the first round.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:08.100
At the same time, you've probably seen some
of the news coverage too.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.500
This year’s games have been embroiled in
controversy, from Qatar’s human rights abuses,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.680
to FIFA corruption, to political conflicts
happening along the sidelines.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:25.010
Soccer - AKA Futbol - is the most popular
sport in the world, and every FIFA World Cup

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.890
final is watched by billions of fans internationally.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.890
Think of it this way: if aliens studied Earth,
they would probably note in their report that

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:39.689
all humans, regardless of where they live,
are driven to fits of passion by 22 people

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:42.210
kicking a ball up and down a field.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.130
There’s simply nothing else like it on the
planet.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.750
But why?

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.460
Why does this game garner such universal attention?

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.369
Why do countries spend billions in order to
host?

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:58.350
And what does the World Cup's power mean when
it is wielded by a country like Qatar?

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.899
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is why it
matters.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:04.840
For today’s episode, I sat down with Laurent
Dubois.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.000
He’s the Academic Director of the Karsh
Institute of Democracy, and a professor at

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.910
the University of Virginia, where he teaches
a course called Soccer Politics.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.390
Enjoy!

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:22.540
Gabrielle SIERRA: I thought it would be appropriate
to open our chat by telling you that I was

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.970
in fact the captain of my high school soccer
team, two years in a row.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.820
We were just truly terrible.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.800
We lost constantly, but I love soccer.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.670
And so in that spirit, we’re an international
relations think tank.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.980
We don’t spend much time discussing sporting
events.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.080
So, why are we talking about the world cup?

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.060
Laurent DUBOIS: The way I put it is that every
World Cup final is watched by more human beings

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.170
than have ever watched anything before in
the history of the human race.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.170
Right?

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.170
Just to put it in context.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.170
SIERRA: Oh, that’s a lot.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:58.010
DUBOIS: So, you do have, I think the achievement
of some kind of universal competition that

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.140
is really shared.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.450
And I think we all know that if you want to
start a conversation probably anywhere in

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.650
the world, you can bring up soccer and the
World Cup and most of the time you will have

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.920
an interesting conversation and people will
have opinions about it.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.420
And I think that's a certain kind of success,
for all the other issues with it, it's worth

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.730
taking stock of that as something pretty remarkable
in our world.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.000
So, the origins of the World Cup, I think
are interesting in that this is a competition

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.950
that emerges in the wake of World War I, out
of a certain sentiment, a post-World-War-I

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.349
sentiment that was very much linked to the
rise of the rules-based international order

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:30.819
or the project of the League of Nations.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.490
The whole idea that, effectively, the world
was seeing a serious, violent breakdown of

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.689
an international set of relationships.

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:42.819
And for those who were boosters of soccer,
they hoped that this kind of sport that was

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:47.200
already well established in many parts of
the world could be a kind of language.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.760
Some people even described it as an Esperanto
at the time.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.560
And the idea would be that understanding that
people were going to continue to be focused

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.810
on their nations and want to represent and
be proud of their nations.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:04.090
But was there a way of doing that, that was
not a war-like relationship, but an engagement

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:05.090
and a set of rules.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.379
SIERRA: Flash forward a century.

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:13.250
The World Cup has not ended war - but it has
become the most popular event on Earth.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:18.840
For decades, it was dominated by two regions:
Europe, and Latin America.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.060
But in 2010, something unexpected happened.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:27.680
FIFA president Sepp Blatter announced that
for the first time in history, the world cup

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.610
would be held in the Middle East, in the tiny
petrostate of Qatar.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.650
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YfyPi5MnPSY
PBS NewsHour: The winner to organize the 2022

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.530
FIFA World Cup is…

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:38.530
Qatar!

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:45.870
SIERRA: The news sent shockwaves through the
world of soccer, and for many fans, it just

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.430
didn’t add up.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:53.260
The host country was not just small, it also
had no sporting infrastructure or domestic

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.500
soccer culture to speak of.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:01.500
Its desert climate reaches temperatures over
100 degrees fahrenheit in the summer, which

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.000
is when the Cup is usually held.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.930
And to top it all off, Qatar’s politics
were at odds with Western democratic values.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:15.990
Allegations of bribery and corruption swirled,
and a long stream of protests began.

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:20.130
At this point, it’s probably worth mentioning
a few things about Qatar itself.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:24.280
For most of its modern history, it had a modest
stature.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.900
At the beginning of the 20th century, its
economy still relied on pearl diving in the

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:29.930
Gulf.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:35.960
But in 1939 it discovered more lucrative resources—oil
and natural gas.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:42.350
By the time Qatar became independent of British
rule in 1971, it had the third largest oil

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.199
reserves in the world.

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:51.310
Now, the country is fabulously wealthy, especially
given its tiny population of 3 million, 88

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.850
percent of which consists of foreigners and
foreign laborers.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:06:00.460
That’s right, there are only about 300,000
native Qataris.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:06.620
Qatar is a semi-constitutional monarchy that
has enshrined sharia in its legal system,

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:11.690
but it nonetheless seems liberal compared
to its neighbors After all it does observe

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:18.099
some elections, and its the home of Al Jazeera,
which is considered the most independent large

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:21.110
media organization in the Muslim world.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:24.870
SIERRA: Let's talk about this year's World
Cup.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:31.349
I've been following along with the World Cup
coverage and so much of it seems incredibly

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:33.449
negative.

00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:37.090
Could you speak on this year's World Cup drama
in particular?

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:40.310
DUBOIS: I mean, there are many things that
are peculiar about this World Cup.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.139
For one thing, it's happening at a different
time of year than the World Cup has ever happened

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:45.139
before.

00:06:45.139 --> 00:06:47.680
So, it's happening in the winter because of
the heat conditions in Qatar.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.750
It is in a small country with a very particular
social matrix and political matrix.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:58.900
It is well known that there have been a lot
of human rights abuses and labor abuses in

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.580
the construction of stadia.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:05.259
So, there's a lot of concern, specifically
about the cost, essentially, of this World

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:06.949
Cup, the human cost.

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:11.599
And there have been a series of corruption
scandals that emerged from the awarding of

00:07:11.599 --> 00:07:16.550
the World Cup to Qatar and the broader question
of how FIFA makes these decisions.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:20.270
At the same time, it's the first World Cup
held in an Arab country, in a Middle Eastern

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.270
country.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:26.110
It comes in the wake of several other World
Cups that have, themselves, been controversial

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.980
in terms of the politics.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:32.370
And so, it feels like it's crystallizing things
that have been building, actually for a while,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.370
around the World Cup.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.729
And the fundamental thing I think is this
kind of feeling of contradiction between what

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:42.390
the World Cup is meant to be, which is a celebration
of human contact and of the joy of the sport

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:46.860
and of people coming from all around, and
then the infrastructural, economic, social

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.110
dynamics of the World Cup.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:52.750
And I think we'll be seeing that a lot as
it unfolds and people, journalists and fans,

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.120
trying to navigate those contradictions in
certain ways.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:00.750
SIERRA: Same sex marriage is illegal in Qatar,
and sex between men is technically punishable

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.690
by prison sentence.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:07.620
This has led to widespread frustration among
LGBTQ groups and their allies.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:13.430
Qatar, for its part, has noted that its laws
are consistent with others across the Arab

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.099
world, and that they are generally not strictly
enforced.

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:20.479
The other major issue has to do with migrant
labor.

00:08:20.479 --> 00:08:26.010
BBC News: There are just days to go before
the start of the World Cup in Qatar but the

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:27.860
build-up to the event has been blighted by
safety concerns.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:32.500
France 24: Six-and-a-half thousand migrant
workers lost their lives in the construction

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:37.160
of the stadiums and infrastructure to enable
soccer to be played in the intense heat.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.409
Channel 4 News: Nirmala Pakrin’s husband,
Rupchandra Rumba, died while he was employed

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:44.350
in one of the new World Cup stadiums in 2019.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:48.500
Rupchandra’s death certificate states he
died of natural causes, a vague term used

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.471
to describe thousands of migrant deaths in
Qatar.

00:08:52.471 --> 00:08:57.970
SIERRA: In order to build World Cup infrastructure
from scratch, Qatar brought in hundreds of

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:03.089
thousands of migrant laborers, mostly from
Africa and South Asia.

00:09:03.089 --> 00:09:08.089
Under the so-called Kafala system, migrant
workers are entirely dependent upon their

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:15.450
employers, leading to unfair or nonexistent
wages, racism, and dangerous working conditions.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:20.660
Reports have found that thousands of such
workers died during preparation for the Cup.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:25.810
Taken together, these issues have led some
observers to declare that the Qatar cup has

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.970
permanently sullied global soccer.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:34.079
Others note that this isn’t the first Cup
to transpire amid human rights problems and

00:09:34.079 --> 00:09:35.450
political debate.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:40.930
DUBOIS: I mean, it's worth reminding people,
the World Cup in 2014 was preceded the previous

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:43.700
year in Brazil with a major social movement.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:48.209
I mean, one that happened during the Confederations
Cup, which is a tournament that happens in

00:09:48.209 --> 00:09:50.170
the place that's hosting the World Cup the
year before.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.019
It's a warm up tournament.

00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:55.860
But it was a massive protest movement and
people really expressed a lot of disgust with

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:00.040
the fact that the Brazilian government had
had to spend so much money to host this tournament

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:03.160
and build infrastructure that wasn't going
to help the people.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.730
So, there have been huge controversies around
this and thinking back to 2018, this was hosted

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:08.769
by Russia.

00:10:08.769 --> 00:10:13.430
Putin presided over the whole thing, certainly
got a lot of help in some ways from hosting

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.680
that tournament, in terms of his image.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.970
Something that now, a few years later, strikes
us again as a problematic thing, I think in

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:21.470
ways that many people recognize.

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:26.040
So, this is not the first time, it may be
just stark in a certain kind of way that I

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:27.399
think is bringing things home.

00:10:27.399 --> 00:10:31.110
SIERRA: The example of Russia stands out in
particular.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:35.770
Just four years ago the world was celebrating
France’s victory in Moscow.

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:40.760
Now Russia is engaged in a brutal war that
aims to change the borders of a democratic

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:41.760
neighbor.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:42.850
Gay marriage?

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.670
Also illegal in Russia.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:49.550
It's a good reminder that the Qatar cup is
only the latest in a series of global events

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:51.790
hosted in the authoritarian world.

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:56.329
SIERRA: Well, in that vein, I want to ask
you about this term sports washing.

00:10:56.329 --> 00:11:00.130
I think it's something that people have heard
the term more and more.

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:05.130
So, do you think that you could just go into
that a little bit and how it pertains to this

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.130
World Cup?

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:10.230
DUBOIS: The most famous case, maybe this is
Adolf Hitler's hosting of the Olympics in

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.519
1936, there were World Cups hosted by Argentina
under dictatorship.

00:11:14.519 --> 00:11:17.220
There have been many other cases where you
can point to this.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:21.500
And the basic idea is that a country that
hosts the World Cup kind of gets to burnish

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:22.500
its image.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.399
It's an opportunity for the country to kind
of be seen by the world in a light that it

00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:27.600
wants, right?

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.209
And to perhaps, sort of, put under the rug
other problems with the country or political

00:11:32.209 --> 00:11:33.209
problems.

00:11:33.209 --> 00:11:34.209
SIERRA: Sure.

00:11:34.209 --> 00:11:36.000
SIERRA: This trend is pretty salient when
it comes to Qatar.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:42.140
Remember, it’s a very small country that
has only been independent since 1971.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:47.500
Qatar has a lot of money, but on the global
stage they tend to be overshadowed by their

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:54.640
larger and more assertive neighbors, especially
Saudi Arabia, Iran, and the United Arab Emirates.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:00.180
For Qatar, the World Cup was a chance to draw
the world’s eye, and to brand themselves.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:04.709
And for this they were not only willing to
pay billions of dollars in infrastructure

00:12:04.709 --> 00:12:09.510
costs, but billions more to develop a global
soccer profile.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:15.040
In 2011, one year after winning their World
Cup bid, Qatar purchased the Paris St. Germain

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:20.120
soccer team, following the lead of other middle
eastern countries like Saudi Arabia and the

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.010
United Arab Emirates.

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:27.470
They proceeded to pour enormous resources
into the team, scooping up world-class stars

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:32.810
like Neymar and Lionel Messi, while purchasing
exclusive media rights to broadcast soccer

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.460
around the world.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:39.499
It has certainly brought them recognition,
but that has also come with scrutiny, and

00:12:39.499 --> 00:12:44.170
accusations that they have soured European
soccer by making it impossible for other teams

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.000
to compete financially.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:51.149
The World Cup will be a big test for how much
of a return they get on their investment.

00:12:51.149 --> 00:12:56.149
DUBOIS: The issue is that it only works to
some extent because of course, what also happens

00:12:56.149 --> 00:12:57.829
is journalists descend on your country.

00:12:57.829 --> 00:13:01.339
SIERRA: I was going to say, doesn't it also
call attention to some of the things?

00:13:01.339 --> 00:13:02.339
DUBOIS: Yes.

00:13:02.339 --> 00:13:03.339
So, it does, in a certain way.

00:13:03.339 --> 00:13:08.220
I mean, I think that it's very clear that
the question of migrant labor and the oppression

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:12.829
of migrant laborers in Qatar for instance,
would likely have not been on many people's

00:13:12.829 --> 00:13:14.570
radar but for the World Cup.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.940
And it's an issue that exists outside the
World Cup and now it's visible.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.440
So, I do think that there's a double-edged
sword to it.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:27.090
We're talking about a larger context in which
the investments in sport have really skyrocketed

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:28.339
over the last couple decades.

00:13:28.339 --> 00:13:31.350
So, there's a lot of questions of also, ‘whose
money is going where?’

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:32.370
in the professional game.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:36.930
And so, I think there's a way in which these
things are interlinked and there's no doubt

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:41.520
that the sport is deeply embedded in all these
other troubling questions.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:45.970
And then at the same time, it can't quite
be contained or controlled by that, because

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:50.019
the thing about it is it's a sport, which
means you have no idea what's going to happen

00:13:50.019 --> 00:13:51.459
on the field.

00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:56.589
You also, and this I think is maybe important
to emphasize, put on the field players, who

00:13:56.589 --> 00:14:01.380
most of the time, in most of their societies,
have often come from pretty marginalized parts

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:02.380
of society.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:03.380
Right?

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:06.640
A lot of athletes are people who have experienced,
to some extent, the worst of a particular

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:07.640
nation.

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:11.270
They have found in sport a form of social
mobility that they couldn't find in other

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:12.270
places.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:16.779
So, many, many of the athletes who become
icons during a World Cup represent the nation

00:14:16.779 --> 00:14:20.850
they come from in a way very different from
the political or economic elites.

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:25.830
And I think that's part of the double-edged
sword too, is that a country's identity gets

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:29.720
represented by people who in many other cases
wouldn't have the ability to represent that

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.590
country in the same way but for the World
Cup.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:36.970
SIERRA: FIFA likes to claim that the World
Cup is apolitical.

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:38.350
I mean, do you think that's true?

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:39.930
DUBOIS: Absolutely not.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:42.170
It's sort of an absurd statement.

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:45.620
What they mean by that is, "We don't want
people to say political things that we don't

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.560
like or that are controversial."

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:52.170
I mean, is it political in the sense that
it's directly related to elections and countries?

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:55.630
Well, not exactly in that sense, but I mean
if you have a slightly larger sense of what's

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:57.120
political, it's obviously political.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:01.760
And if you ask yourself, “when do you get
to see your nation in some sense, outside

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:03.639
of just an abstraction?”

00:15:03.639 --> 00:15:06.839
You can see the president of the country,
you can see the parliament, you maybe see

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:09.089
the armed forces, you see the flag.

00:15:09.089 --> 00:15:10.769
But those are a little bit more abstract.

00:15:10.769 --> 00:15:11.769
SIERRA: Right.

00:15:11.769 --> 00:15:15.570
DUBOIS: Whereas, the national team just feels
like for a little while you're just looking

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:16.800
at Brazil.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.570
And everyone knows you're probably not going
to win the World Cup, but maybe you have a

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.920
good run, you have a couple games.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.910
People celebrate just any World Cup victory.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:29.811
I mean, just an example, the first game of
the 2018 World Cup, Mexico-Germany, Mexico

00:15:29.811 --> 00:15:35.350
defeated Germany, and the earthquake monitoring
division in Mexico City registered a small

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:40.079
earthquake when the goal was scored and when
the game ended, just from people jumping up

00:15:40.079 --> 00:15:41.079
and down in Mexico City.

00:15:41.079 --> 00:15:42.079
SIERRA: Oh my god, really?

00:15:42.079 --> 00:15:45.120
DUBOIS: That wasn't winning the World Cup,
that was just the first game of the group

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:46.120
stage-

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.120
SIERRA: Aww, that's amazing.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:48.540
DUBOIS: But it was a national event that everyone
will remember.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:53.290
So, there's a way in which, on that level,
I guess it's joyously political in a way that

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.319
we sometimes don't think of.

00:15:54.319 --> 00:15:55.790
SIERRA: I like that, joyously political.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:56.790
DUBOIS: Yeah.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:16:01.700
SIERRA: I mean, sometimes teams and matches
in the World Cup are seen as symbols for geopolitical

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:03.440
changes and tensions.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:09.170
So, are there any teams out there that symbolize
a changing world for you?

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:12.209
DUBOIS: There's several powerful examples
in this World Cup.

00:16:12.209 --> 00:16:17.470
Probably the Iranian team is one of the most
clear, because there are, obviously given

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:20.579
the situation in Iran and the protests in
Iran, there are many athletes who have started

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:23.040
to express support for the protests.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:27.310
Some are, it seems, not being included perhaps,
or punished in a certain way.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:31.370
This happened in 2009 too, where athletes
on the Iranian football team supported the

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:33.500
2009 protests in Iran.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:38.791
So, there you really see a deep tension, which
is in some ways the Iranian team is an official

00:16:38.791 --> 00:16:43.380
team by the federation and therefore in a
sense a kind of governmental representative.

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:47.339
I mean not a formal governmental representative
but they do represent the country.

00:16:47.339 --> 00:16:51.170
And yet, I think for a lot of the players,
they think they are representing the Iranian

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:54.660
people who are right now, many of them, in
revolt against their government.

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:58.569
So, I think that's an opportunity for players,
but it's also an opportunity the government

00:16:58.569 --> 00:16:59.569
wants to contain.

00:16:59.569 --> 00:17:03.730
Sky Sports News: Now before the game, none
of the Iran players sang the national anthem

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:06.310
of the Islamic Republic of Iran.

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:11.870
The captain, Ehsan Hajsafi, said before the
tournament that the national team supports

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:15.339
those who have died in protests across the
country.

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:19.630
DUBOIS: A case that I've written about a lot,
of course, is the French national team and

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:23.419
you can think of other European teams where
you have teams that are actually majority

00:17:23.419 --> 00:17:25.760
made up of children of immigrants, often.

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:33.370
And so, who are representing Europe but also
representing home nations, or represent multiple

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:34.380
identities.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:38.350
And at times you've had people attack those
teams for not representing the country.

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:39.350
SIERRA: Oh, interesting.

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:40.840
DUBOIS: Not representing France.

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:44.059
And of course, they do represent France, they
represent maybe just a different version of

00:17:44.059 --> 00:17:45.650
France than other people want to be seen.

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:46.650
SIERRA: Of course.

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:50.510
DUBOIS: We have a US player, Chris Matthews,
who's very strongly invested in Black Lives

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:55.780
Matter and anti-racist activism, and a lot
of players around the world who also speak

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:59.720
up about essentially the issues in their home
countries that they're passionate about.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:01.110
Athletes can do that in all kinds of settings.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:05.090
But when you are representing your country
in the most watched sporting event on the

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:08.690
planet, you do have a different kind of platform
that's just on another level.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:12.550
SIERRA: At the same time, all of these players
are here to win right?

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:17.360
I mean that is what most viewers are expecting
to see when they tune in, like just a good

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:18.380
soccer match.

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:22.470
DUBOIS: I think that there's a way in which
the World Cup gives us a break from other

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:23.470
things, right?

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:26.660
Notably from the political conflicts in our
countries, from the news and everything that's

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:27.840
happening in our world.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:31.770
It's going to be a time where we can maybe
forget about all that for a while.

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:35.180
And then at the same time, it plunges us right
back into geopolitics, right?

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:39.460
We're thinking about nations and their relationship,
but maybe refracted in a slightly different

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:40.460
way.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:43.320
And I think that's part of the point, is that
when we're thinking about our nations and

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:46.520
then thinking about the larger world through
the World Cup, maybe it's giving us a more

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:48.410
hopeful way of thinking about that.

00:18:48.410 --> 00:18:52.669
I think at the best you can say, can we take
some of that more positive energy about unity

00:18:52.669 --> 00:18:57.940
and connection, even affection across differences,
and then bring that into our political lives.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:02.070
That's what would be the most hopeful version
of what can happen in that context.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:08.571
SIERRA: So, going from beautiful and inclusive
and amazing, to FIFA, because we have to talk

00:19:08.571 --> 00:19:10.370
about FIFA for a moment.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:15.590
I read a piece that said, "FIFA is actually
more powerful than the UN."

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:18.159
Which seemed maybe impossible to me.

00:19:18.159 --> 00:19:20.220
I mean, what's up with FIFA?

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:25.040
DUBOIS: Well, it's such a fascinating, international
organization, and in a sense, I don't think

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:29.760
people have analyzed it enough, just from
a political science institutional background.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:32.870
They say they’re more important, partly
because there's actually more members than

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:37.809
the UN, because there are places that are
members of FIFA that aren't countries, Palestine,

00:19:37.809 --> 00:19:41.350
Caledonia, obviously, the Faroe Islands, et
cetera.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:42.640
You can mention various ones.

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:44.870
So, they have a larger footprint, I guess.

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:48.670
There are nations that are in FIFA that aren't
recognized as nations otherwise, in that sense.

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:55.100
This is a, originally, a cute, little organization
organized mainly in Europe to organize games

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:56.250
between countries.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:20:02.530
And then it grew, especially through the '60s
and '70s, it grew as decolonization happened.

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:06.480
And one of the first things that countries
would do after getting independence would

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:09.210
be, of course, join the UN and also join FIFA.

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:10.210
Right?

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:14.780
As it grew, the power of FIFA grew more and
more, because FIFA can determine a lot of

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:15.780
flows of money.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.169
They decide, of course, where tournaments
are hosted, not just the big ones but small

00:20:19.169 --> 00:20:20.169
ones.

00:20:20.169 --> 00:20:21.730
And each of those tournaments brings revenue.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:25.630
They now control the Women's World Cup and
Women's International Tournament, after many

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.169
years of not being interested, but in the
'90s they started being interested.

00:20:29.169 --> 00:20:34.280
But the real thing is the nexus that happened
in the '80s and '90s when television rights

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:40.490
became privatized and the money flowing into
particular viewing rights became massive,

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:45.830
is that you just have this organization that
controls a nexus of institutional, structural,

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:50.810
and economic power, in a way that does mirror
the UN, in the sense that there's a general

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:55.470
assembly, but then there's this group of people,
group of countries who really have a lot more

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:56.470
control.

00:20:56.470 --> 00:21:03.090
PBS NewsHour: People who have even a casual
understanding of what FIFA represents associate

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:07.120
it with corruption, and cronyism, and a lack
of transparency.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:13.420
WeShow Football: But in no way can FIFA accept
to be responsible for the welfare of workers.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:16.320
They belong to commercial and industrial companies
of other countries.

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:22.200
Wall Street Journal: The upshot has been a
radical reconfiguration of soccer’s power

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:23.200
structure.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:27.240
A president can be elected without a single
vote from Europe and South America confederations.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:32.460
Real Stories: In order to win a bid to host
the World Cup you have to convince a majority

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:36.350
of the 24 voters on the FIFA executive committee.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:40.679
They are the gods of football.

00:21:40.679 --> 00:21:44.900
DUBOIS: And then, people are not as familiar
with this, but there's then the board related

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:48.951
to FIFA, the International Football Association
Board that governs the rules of soccer, which

00:21:48.951 --> 00:21:53.460
has a weird structure in that four of those
eight seats are actually just England, Scotland,

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:54.789
Wales, and Northern Ireland.

00:21:54.789 --> 00:21:55.789
SIERRA: Oh, okay.

00:21:55.789 --> 00:22:00.890
DUBOIS: So, half of that is controlled basically
just by the UK, and then the other four are

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:01.890
rotating.

00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:05.090
But in terms of the specific issues, which
is that there is just a huge amount of opportunity

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:10.049
for corruption within FIFA, and who is the
body above FIFA that would police FIFA?

00:22:10.049 --> 00:22:11.590
Well, there isn't really one.

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:15.950
And the real question is, does FIFA represent
football fans and players around the world?

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:20.050
Well, not exactly, it really more represents
a set of interests within them,

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:22.010
SIERRA: Let's talk about money.

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:27.820
So, how much money is at play when FIFA selects
a new World Cup host?

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:30.620
DUBOIS: It's really a weird relationship,
right?

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:36.299
Because the country takes on all of the responsibility
for the infrastructure.

00:22:36.299 --> 00:22:38.919
This was the issue in Brazil and in South
Africa.

00:22:38.919 --> 00:22:42.580
It's not like FIFA gives the country a bunch
of money to build the stadium.

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:47.880
They just require them to build a bunch of
stadia, according to rather specific rules,

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:48.880
actually.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:53.770
Then FIFA actually totally controls what happens
in the stadia and who gets to sell alcohol

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:54.770
and stuff like that.

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:59.380
SIERRA: Side note, as you may have heard,
Qatar banned alcohol sales in stadia at the

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:00.840
last minute.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:06.370
This was bad news for many fans, and also
for World Cup sponsor Budweiser, which eventually

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:10.670
decided to award all unsold beer to the victorious
country.

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:14.200
DUBOIS: Food is always really bad at World
Cup games.

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:17.960
You're in South Africa, but you just have
to eat a bad hot dog.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:19.760
So, it's a weird relationship.

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.700
Now, what the country gets in principle, right?

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:26.340
Is people coming to the World Cup, the money
from tourism and those sorts of things.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:30.650
On balance, I think when people have really
studied it, it tends to be actually at a loss.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:35.179
The countries tend to have to spend more money
than they get from people visiting, just because

00:23:35.179 --> 00:23:39.451
it just doesn't pan out, even if you really
gouge people on their hotels and stuff, which

00:23:39.451 --> 00:23:40.650
places do.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:44.780
I think the reason that politicians go for
it is not really because - they'll argue that

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:48.450
it's good financially for the country, but
really what they're thinking about is themselves.

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:50.800
It's good for them to host this World Cup.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:55.929
So, it's sort of a losing proposition from
a raw financial perspective, for a country,

00:23:55.929 --> 00:23:56.929
generally.

00:23:56.929 --> 00:24:00.040
I mean, it does create employment and you
can argue a bunch of different ways about

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:01.040
it.

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:03.530
But I think again, it's more that symbolic
draw.

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:07.110
People are drawn to it like moths to the flame
that they have to be hosting the World Cup.

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:10.900
And of course, it depends on - like when the
United States, Canada, and Mexico host the

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:17.169
World Cup in 2026, to some extent, the infrastructural
investment is going to be less of a big deal

00:24:17.169 --> 00:24:23.380
on some level than it was say, in South Africa,
and Brazil, and Qatar, because there's already

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:25.620
a lot of big stadia, et cetera.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:30.419
SIERRA: To put things in perspective, the
2022 world cup is astronomically more expensive

00:24:30.419 --> 00:24:32.010
than previous years.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:36.039
Qatar has spent more than twice the previous
8 cups combined.

00:24:36.039 --> 00:24:44.299
In 2018, Russia spent 11.6 billion, and in
2014, the cup cost Brazil 15 billion.

00:24:44.299 --> 00:24:49.170
Typically, countries don’t recoup the costs
immediately, instead they hope the tournament

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:52.419
will have a positive effect on their economy
in the long run.

00:24:52.419 --> 00:24:59.870
Qatar, for example, only expects to make back
17 billion dollars of the 220 billion it shelled

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:05.779
out in the short term, and South Africa is
estimated to have lost 2.8 billion when it

00:25:05.779 --> 00:25:07.990
hosted in 2010.

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:09.830
Who makes the immediate cash?

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:10.830
FIFA.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:15.640
Each world cup rakes in billions of dollars
through TV and marketing rights.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:21.450
SIERRA: It just makes me think of all of the
times that we see the photos of the almost

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:25.420
ruins of the stadiums that they built after
the game.

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:30.049
Whether it's from the Olympics or the World
Cup, I mean it seems like definitely a loss,

00:25:30.049 --> 00:25:31.049
not a gain.

00:25:31.049 --> 00:25:32.049
DUBOIS: Yeah.

00:25:32.049 --> 00:25:35.080
In Brazil, I mean the only people who can
fill some of those stadias are now these big

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:38.570
evangelical rallies, which bring together
80,000 people sometimes.

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:42.710
But yeah, there's no local league that has
enough people to go in those stadia.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:46.770
SIERRA: So, corruption keeps coming up, you’ve
mentioned it a few times already.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:49.530
So can you tell me a bit more about that?

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:54.210
DUBOIS: What we're really talking about is
that there are groups of elites or oligarchs

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:59.451
in a particular country, who are deeply connected
with the people who run FIFA and who make

00:25:59.451 --> 00:26:00.789
arrangements.

00:26:00.789 --> 00:26:04.490
And on both sides, a lot of people are making
a lot of money from that, right?

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:08.100
So, the government officials who decide to
host the World Cup are also often tied to

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:12.120
the contracts and can give contracts to their
friends to build the hotels.

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.950
It's not necessarily like someone handed a
suitcase of money in a back alley to someone,

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:16.950
right?

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:17.950
SIERRA: Right.

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:18.950
With a wink.

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:19.950
Yeah.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:22.710
DUBOIS: It's more just that these things are
so intertwined and the people who decide who's

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:26.830
going to get the TV rights in a particular
country, and which country's going to host

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:27.830
it.

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:30.870
I think it's that corruption that happens
when there's almost no transparency.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:36.070
Again, what is the body that's going to really
investigate FIFA in a way that is solid?

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:40.390
I mean, even when the Swiss police and the
FBI joined together, they made certain things

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:44.250
stick, but it's actually really hard to investigate
a body like that.

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:49.049
SIERRA: Rumors of corruption have only grown
in the decades leading up to the current World

00:26:49.049 --> 00:26:50.049
Cup.

00:26:50.049 --> 00:26:55.880
The main accusation is that top soccer officials
within FIFA were bribed into voting for Qatar.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.710
Numerous investigations have been conducted,
and the US department of justice even indicted

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:06.169
16 officials in 2015 for racketeering conspiracy,
and corruption.

00:27:06.169 --> 00:27:11.940
Later that year, FIFA president Sepp Blatter
resigned, in part due to the ongoing scandal.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:18.090
FIFA, for its part, has denied accusations
of bribery, noting that no irrefutable evidence

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:19.090
has been produced.

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:24.090
They have also emphasized that “reform takes
time”.

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:29.340
In the meantime, the FIFA bidding process,
for all of its global impact, remains veiled

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:30.340
in secrecy.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:36.790
DUBOIS: So, they deploy a ton of power without
much transparency, and then brush off the

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:40.340
idea that there should be transparency by
saying that they're not really that important,

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:41.340
in some ways.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:46.100
And all of it comes from the fact that they're
controlling something that people around the

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:49.130
world just love and are going to watch, basically.

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:52.450
It's very difficult to convince people not
to watch the World Cup.

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:56.559
There's some courageous French towns that
have said they're not putting up big screens,

00:27:56.559 --> 00:28:00.850
to boycott this World Cup because of the human
rights abuses.

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:04.990
But I think most people in those towns will
still watch the French games in their houses.

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:08.940
Its just like you can’t really stop people
going for this.

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:15.019
So, FIFA knows that on some level and no one
in FIFA is elected by their countries.

00:28:15.019 --> 00:28:17.320
There's not a way to control them.

00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:21.000
And I think that’s part of it so, if you
believe that human beings are often drawn

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:25.650
to corruption when they're not controlled
by some structure, FIFA's a good case of that.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:30.350
I mean, in the US case, like with the Sally
Yates report that just came about, about sexual

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:34.570
harassment and violence in women's soccer
here, you have a federation that finally after

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:38.830
really not dealing with an issue, I think
is playing an important role in addressing

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:40.549
systemic questions in the country.

00:28:40.549 --> 00:28:46.480
SIERRA: Released this past October, the Yates
report exposed extensive sexual and emotional

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:52.940
abuse perpetrated by former FIFA employees
within the women’s professional soccer league.

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:55.640
DUBOIS: These federations are nonprofit organizations.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:58.299
They're supposed to sustain people who play
soccer in their country.

00:28:58.299 --> 00:29:02.180
So, they could and should be forces for good
in their countries, and in some cases they

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:03.590
are more than others.

00:29:03.590 --> 00:29:05.919
But I think people deserve better organizations
than they have.

00:29:05.919 --> 00:29:06.919
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:29:06.919 --> 00:29:09.220
I mean, all this said, are you going to watch
the World Cup?

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:10.220
DUBOIS: Absolutely.

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:11.220
Yeah.

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:12.220
I'm falling right into that category.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:13.220
Yeah.

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:17.340
I wish I could deliver a more morally cogent
statement.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:22.220
I mean, I do think that, like in lots of other
realms of our lives, there are organisms that

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:26.159
we maybe don't like the way they're run, we
don't believe in all their choices, and we

00:29:26.159 --> 00:29:27.539
participate in, nonetheless.

00:29:27.539 --> 00:29:30.980
I think we're all part of institutions in
one way or another, or social institutions,

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:32.049
that are like that.

00:29:32.049 --> 00:29:36.820
It feels like, to me anyway, there's still
something that is very unique about this event,

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:41.630
maybe in a time like now more than ever, where
I do believe in that initial romantic nature

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:46.010
of the World Cup, that there is something
powerful about human beings being united in

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:47.010
this.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:51.500
And if we can get a little bit of that and
use that energy to do better as a planet and

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:54.309
as a world, I'm for that.

00:29:54.309 --> 00:29:58.309
I also think that people need to call out
the injustices and speak out about what's

00:29:58.309 --> 00:29:59.309
done.

00:29:59.309 --> 00:30:02.030
There isn't a reason why things had to be
done the way they were in Qatar for building

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:05.050
these, they could have paid laborers better,
they could have treated people better.

00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:07.690
And so, I think it's worth calling those things
out as well.

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:12.289
SIERRA: Whenever I think of the World Cup,
I think of, especially lately, just so many

00:30:12.289 --> 00:30:18.799
people coming together, rooting for one team,
and I guess once the US is out, another team,

00:30:18.799 --> 00:30:22.590
but all in the same bar, all cheering for
the same thing.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:27.370
And aside from the Olympics and even then,
I don't know anything as powerful as that.

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:29.519
DUBOIS: There just isn't anything else.

00:30:29.519 --> 00:30:32.770
And people do, they root for other countries.

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:34.120
There's an interesting openness, right?

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:36.179
You learn about other countries.

00:30:36.179 --> 00:30:39.320
At the beginning of the World Cup, you've
never heard of a particular country and then

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:42.880
maybe you fall in love with that country just
because you love the way their team plays.

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:45.870
Suddenly you're super into Croatia.

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:49.539
And I think that’s education, that’s global
education for people.

00:30:49.539 --> 00:30:53.530
It does affect the way that you see your country
and your community, and maybe makes you open

00:30:53.530 --> 00:30:55.920
in different ways and think about your nation
in a different way.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:59.510
So, I think all those things are very powerful
and there isn't really any other thing that

00:30:59.510 --> 00:31:02.190
delivers it like the World Cup.

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:06.070
SIERRA: Alright, let’s end on a philosophical
note then.

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:07.620
Why soccer?

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:10.360
Why not basketball or baseball?

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:12.399
What made soccer the world’s game?

00:31:12.399 --> 00:31:15.139
DUBOIS: It is this really interesting question,
right?

00:31:15.139 --> 00:31:19.600
Obviously, sports are a huge part of human
culture all across the world, but soccer just

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:23.670
stands out in the sense that it is present
pretty much everywhere.

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:28.409
It comes out of a particularly specific place
and time, but then it spread so quickly to

00:31:28.409 --> 00:31:33.200
Latin America, to Africa, to Asia, and by
the end of the 19th, beginning of the 20th

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:36.470
century, it's already becoming this kind of
global language.

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:38.809
And I think the question I'm always thinking
about is why?

00:31:38.809 --> 00:31:44.450
What is it about this particular form that
allowed so many people to embrace it?

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:49.570
I think there are specific things actually
about the nature of the game that allows both

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:53.760
something universal, something that people
share across cultures, which makes it a kind

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:55.470
of language, and that's attractive.

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:59.920
And at the same time, it allows, I think for
people to express very local identities, local

00:31:59.920 --> 00:32:02.580
cultures, local ways of being through the
sport.

00:32:02.580 --> 00:32:07.070
So, it enables people to both be very rooted
in the way that they play the sport and also

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:10.600
be playing something that they know is shared
across the whole world.

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:13.120
And you can have funny conversations about
this in the United States, because there's

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:14.120
people who love soccer.

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:17.620
There's also people who just really hate it,
who find it boring, incomprehensible.

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:19.929
They don't understand what the thing is.

00:32:19.929 --> 00:32:21.399
"Why is the game 0-0?

00:32:21.399 --> 00:32:22.960
No one ever scores a goal.

00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:24.250
They're flopping around."

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:28.281
We have all heard the critiques and had fun
conversations with soccer haters, and all

00:32:28.281 --> 00:32:29.780
legitimate critiques.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:31.659
People love their sports for different reasons.

00:32:31.659 --> 00:32:34.960
But I guess what I would lean into is that
a lot of the things that people who dislike

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:40.070
this sport call attention to as its problems,
I think are actually its virtues.

00:32:40.070 --> 00:32:45.179
And what I mean by that is that a lot of the
things about the game create a very emotionally

00:32:45.179 --> 00:32:50.970
complex relationship to the game, in that
it is very true that the outcomes of the games

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:53.220
often just don't really feel fair.

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:55.769
They don't really feel like they reflect what
should have happened.

00:32:55.769 --> 00:32:58.570
Some kind of stupid mistake by someone.

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:02.380
One instant the game can be going really well
for a team and then they make a stupid mistake

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:03.510
and they lose because of that.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:08.690
The referees, of course, notoriously, are
dealing with lots of gray areas with offside

00:33:08.690 --> 00:33:14.429
calls, with handball calls, with different
kinds of calls that even in the era of VAR

00:33:14.429 --> 00:33:18.059
and video assistants, still remain ambiguous
and people argue about them forever.

00:33:18.059 --> 00:33:22.810
I have friends who still discuss refereeing
calls from the 1982 World Cup, as if it just

00:33:22.810 --> 00:33:23.810
happened yesterday.

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:24.810
Right?

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:27.669
So, it's actually one of the things that people
love doing about the game actually, is to

00:33:27.669 --> 00:33:32.160
talk endlessly about basically the system
of justice and how it misfired, and how it's

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:33.399
unfair to them.

00:33:33.399 --> 00:33:37.649
And then coupled with that, you just have
moments of beauty and glory and people who

00:33:37.649 --> 00:33:40.610
carry out a set of plays that are just incredible
to behold.

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:45.549
So, you have this mix of things that you're
watching, that I think just really reflect

00:33:45.549 --> 00:33:47.059
people's lived experience, right?

00:33:47.059 --> 00:33:52.529
The mix of possibility and joy and beauty,
and then stupid things happening to you, basically,

00:33:52.529 --> 00:33:53.529
unfair things.

00:33:53.529 --> 00:33:54.600
SIERRA: Which is universal.

00:33:54.600 --> 00:33:55.639
DUBOIS: Yeah, exactly.

00:33:55.639 --> 00:33:56.639
Unfair things happening.

00:33:56.639 --> 00:34:00.580
Being the best at what you do and still somebody
mediocre gets the job, or something, right?

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:01.580
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:02.580
Right.

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:04.190
DUBOIS: So, I think people relate to that,
actually.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:07.210
So the game gives you a kind of language to
think about life.

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:11.240
And then that basic core thing, which is then
having to pick yourself up even in the face

00:34:11.240 --> 00:34:15.550
of injustice and unfairness and get the energy
to play the next game.

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:18.960
And the one thing that the game offers and
that the World Cup offers is it's going to

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:19.970
keep happening.

00:34:19.970 --> 00:34:24.129
But you have no guarantee, in soccer, that
working hard and doing all the things right

00:34:24.129 --> 00:34:25.419
is going to lead to the outcome you want.

00:34:25.419 --> 00:34:26.419
SIERRA: Wow.

00:34:26.419 --> 00:34:27.820
I love that you're looking at that as a positive.

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:28.950
DUBOIS: Yeah.

00:34:28.950 --> 00:34:34.010
Well, I think the point is that it's like,
you can want to see sports as a place that's

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:36.089
outside of normal experience.

00:34:36.089 --> 00:34:40.040
Which is fair enough, like a place where things
are different from the world and there may

00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:41.669
be more fair than the world.

00:34:41.669 --> 00:34:44.399
That's not something you're going to really
get in soccer, actually.

00:34:44.399 --> 00:34:50.609
SIERRA: There’s no question that some aspects
of the Qatar cup are queasy.

00:34:50.609 --> 00:34:55.159
The stadiums were built on the backs of exploited
migrant labor.

00:34:55.159 --> 00:35:01.050
LGBTQ fans have been told to ‘tone it down’
if they attend in person.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:03.310
There is evidence of corruption.

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:09.290
And, in a year when climate effects grew even
worse, it just seems strange to hold the Cup

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:10.780
in a wealthy petrostate.

00:35:10.780 --> 00:35:15.430
With all this, it’s understandable that
rights groups and others are frustrated when

00:35:15.430 --> 00:35:18.930
fans tune out the noise and enjoy the game.

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:21.119
But there’s another way to look at it.

00:35:21.119 --> 00:35:27.160
At a time in history where everything seems
to keep falling apart, the world still has

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:32.930
this funny little game that commands a shared
feeling from billions the world over.

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:38.520
No matter the host country, no matter the
circumstances, the enthusiasm is irrepressible.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:47.780
And maybe it’s good to remember that’s
possible, if only every four years.

